 ### ClipGS
-ClipGS is a Gaussian Splatting-based framework designed for the real-time interactive cinematic visualization of volumetric medical data with support for dynamic clipping planes [S1]. It solves the problem of maintaining rendering quality and continuity when a user interactsively clips a 3D volume, addressing the artifacts caused by hard visibility truncations and the high computational cost of learning full deformation fields for the additional clipping dimension [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [WildGaussians](6064-wildgaussians.md) or [Dynamic and Temporal Modeling](9265-dynamic-and-temporal-modeling.md) by treating the clipping plane not as a temporal dimension requiring a dense deformation field, but as a geometric visibility constraint managed via a learnable truncation scheme and an Adaptive Adjustment Model (AAM) [S1].
+Treats the clipping plane as a geometric visibility constraint managed via a learnable truncation scheme and Adaptive Adjustment Model (AAM) for interactive cinematic visualization of volumetric medical data [9546-clipgs.md].
 
 ### [Representation and Kernel Modifications](10145-representation-and-kernel-modifications.md)
-This family encompasses modifications to the 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representation and rendering kernels to improve geometric fidelity, handle complex visual phenomena (such as shadows or defocus), and enable surface reconstruction. Unlike [Base 3DGS Optimization](7549-base-3d-gaussian-splatting.md), which relies on standard positive Gaussians and exponential kernels, these methods introduce negative primitives, learnable voxel scaffolds, alternative radial basis functions, or oriented surface constraints. They address specific limitations of the base method, such as the inability to represent high-frequency color transitions, sparse initialization artifacts, or the lack of watertight geometry.
+Alters the mathematical definition of the splat (e.g., negative coefficients, voxel scaffolding, alternative radial basis functions) rather than just the optimization schedule [10145-representation-and-kernel-modifications.md].
 
 ### [TideGS Trajectory Ordering](10176-tidegs-trajectory-ordering.md)
-TideGS Trajectory Ordering is a training strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that replaces standard randomized view shuffling with a trajectory-aware ordered sequence of views to improve optimization stability and convergence for large-scale scenes. Unlike standard 3DGS which samples views with random shuffling, TideGS uses a clustered traveling-salesperson (TSP) ordering to preserve trajectory continuity, reducing gradient variation between consecutive steps. It builds on [Base 3DGS Optimization](7549-base-3d-gaussian-splatting-optimization.md) by modifying the view sampling schedule while retaining the standard masked update mechanism.
+Replaces randomized view shuffling with a trajectory-aware ordered sequence (clustered TSP) to improve optimization stability and convergence for large-scale scenes [10176-tidegs-trajectory-ordering.md].
 
 ### [Gradient Computation and Back-Propagation](10202-gradient-computation-and-back-propagation.md)
-Gradient Computation and Back-Propagation defines the mathematical and algorithmic mechanisms for propagating loss gradients from rendered pixels back to the parameters of 3D Gaussian primitives (position, scale, rotation, opacity, color) and camera intrinsics/extrinsics. It solves the problem of optimizing radiance fields by enabling end-to-end training via automatic differentiation or custom analytical backward passes. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by focusing on the core differentiability of the rendering equation, whereas siblings like [Physical Parameter Optimization](4111-physical-parameter-optimization.md) or [WildGaussians](6064-wildgaussians.md) extend or modify this core gradient path for specific domains (e.g., ToF, semantic features, or dynamic scenes).
+Defines the mathematical mechanisms for propagating loss gradients from rendered pixels back to Gaussian parameters and camera poses, focusing on the core differentiability of the rendering equation [10202-gradient-computation-and-back-propagation.md].
 
 ### [VisibilitySplat](10927-visibilitysplat.md)
-VisibilitySplat is a two-stage inverse rendering pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that augments the standard rasterized pipeline with a sparse visibility oracle to estimate transmittance-weighted occlusion for Gaussian primitives from sampled lighting directions, enabling material and light decomposition. It addresses the limitation of standard 3DGS rasterization, which provides limited visibility information regarding cast shadows and mutual occlusion, by integrating a BVH-accelerated point-based ray tracer. Unlike NeRF-based methods that use implicit volumetric formulations, VisibilitySplat uses explicit point primitives via differentiable rasterization for real-time rendering while providing robust visibility signals for stabilizing material-light disentanglement under unknown illumination.
+Augments the rasterized pipeline with a sparse visibility oracle (BVH-accelerated ray tracer) to estimate transmittance-weighted occlusion for material and light decomposition [10927-visibilitysplat.md].
 
 ### [MCMC-based 3D Gaussian Splatting](11914-mcmc-based-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)
-This method reformulates 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) training as a Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) process, replacing the heuristic-based densification and pruning of [Base 3DGS Optimization](7549-base-3dgs-optimization.md) with a stochastic exploration mechanism. It solves the problem of sensitivity to initialization and local minima by allowing Gaussians to explore the scene space via noise injection and relocation, rather than relying on fixed thresholds for cloning and splitting. Unlike [Base 3DGS Optimization](7549-base-3d-gaussian-splatting.md), which uses deterministic heuristics, this approach is robust to random initialization and prevents "stray" Gaussians from diverging into empty space through specific regularizers.
+Reforms training as a Markov Chain Monte Carlo process, replacing heuristic densification with stochastic exploration via noise injection and relocation [11914-mcmc-based-3d-gaussian-splatting.md].
 
 ### [3D Gaussian Splatting as Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC)](11915-3d-gaussian-splatting-as-markov-chain-monte-carlo-mcmc.md)
-This method reinterprets the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting as a sampling process from a target distribution $\mathcal{G}$, rather than a deterministic minimization of a loss function via gradient descent. By framing the optimization as Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC), it enables discrete structural changes (moving, cloning, pruning Gaussians) to be treated as valid state transitions that preserve probability mass, avoiding the discontinuities that break standard gradient-based assumptions. It differs from [Base 3DGS Optimization](7549-base-3d-gaussian-splatting-optimization.md) by injecting anisotropic noise into the mean updates and rewriting heuristics as MCMC moves, and from [Physical Parameter Optimization](4111-physical-parameter-optimization.md) by focusing on the probabilistic interpretation of the training dynamics rather than physical constraints.
+Reinterprets training as sampling from a target distribution, treating discrete structural changes as valid state transitions to avoid discontinuities in gradient-based assumptions [11915-3d-gaussian-splatting-as-markov-chain-monte-carlo-mcmc.md].
 
 ### [Sliced Wasserstein-based Gaussian Cloning Update](11919-sliced-wasserstein-based-gaussian-cloning-update.md)
-A method to determine the scale update for cloned Gaussians by minimizing the difference between the integrated color of the new and old Gaussians along arbitrary 1D slices passing through their centers, ensuring rasterization integral conservation. It sets itself apart from standard densification by enforcing a specific integral constraint on 1D projections rather than relying solely on position gradient magnitudes, leading to improved PSNR, SSIM, and lower LPIPS compared to base 3DGS.
+Determines scale updates for cloned Gaussians by minimizing the difference between integrated color along arbitrary 1D slices, ensuring rasterization integral conservation [11919-sliced-wasserstein-based-gaussian-cloning-update.md].
 
 ### [Cross-Structure Collaboration Module](12071-cross-structure-collaboration-module.md)
-The Cross-Structure Collaboration Module (CSCM) is a feature extraction mechanism for 3D Gaussian Splatting that integrates global tri-plane features with local context-aware grid features to generate Hierarchical Dense Embedded (HDE) features. It solves the problem of feature misalignment and insufficient geometric constraints in large-scale scenes by preserving global coherence while capturing local detail. It sets itself apart from standard tri-plane methods by adding a context grid interpolation step and from pure grid methods by maintaining a static global tri-plane backbone.
+Integrates global tri-plane features with local context-aware grid features to generate Hierarchical Dense Embedded (HDE) features, solving feature misalignment in large-scale scenes [12071-cross-structure-collaboration-module.md].
 
 ### HUGS
-HUGS creates a coarse-to-fine deformation module using linear blend skinning and local learning-based refinement for constructing and animating virtual human avatars based on 3DGS [S1]. It utilizes the tri-plane representation to factorize the canonical space, allowing reconstruction of the person and scene from monocular video (50-100 frames) within 30 minutes [S1]. It achieves state-of-the-art human neural rendering performance at 20 FPS [S1].
+Creates a coarse-to-fine deformation module using linear blend skinning and local learning-based refinement for virtual human avatars, utilizing tri-plane representation to factorize canonical space [S1].
 
 ### Wolf et al. Stereo Method
-Wolf et al. train a 3DGS to render stereo-calibrated novel views [S1]. They apply stereo depth estimation on the rendered views and fuse the estimated dense depth maps together by the Truncated Signed Distance Function (TSDF) to form a triangular mesh [S1].
+Trains 3DGS to render stereo-calibrated views, applies stereo depth estimation, and fuses dense depth maps via Truncated Signed Distance Function (TSDF) to form a triangular mesh [S1].
 
 ### Pseudo Ground Truth Acquisition for Gaussians
-A labeling pipeline that generates Gaussian-specific pseudo ground truth labels from original point cloud labels, accounting for the densification and pruning processes of 3D-GS optimization [S8, unofficial].
+Generates Gaussian-specific pseudo ground truth labels from original point cloud labels, accounting for densification and pruning processes [S8, unofficial].
 
 ### Cross-View Pruning Mechanism
-A mechanism comprising a Cross-View Consistency Loss and adaptive pruning to reduce Gaussian redundancy and ensure geometric validity [S9, unofficial]. It selects adjacent view pairs from $M$ views based on SSIM > 0.6, computes Cross-View Consistent Loss ($L_{CVC}$) to penalize discrepancies between residuals of paired views, and prunes based on depth/distance consistency [S9, unofficial].
+Uses a Cross-View Consistency Loss and adaptive pruning to reduce Gaussian redundancy and ensure geometric validity by selecting adjacent view pairs with SSIM > 0.6 [S9, unofficial].
 
 ### Structure-View Co-learning
-A module combining structural and view-dependent gradients for robust joint optimization of Gaussian positions and appearance [S9, unofficial]. The loss is $\mathcal{L} = \lambda (\lambda_V \mathcal{L}_V + \lambda_S \mathcal{L}_S)$, where $\mathcal{L}_V$ is view gradient loss and $\mathcal{L}_S$ is structure gradient loss [S9, unofficial].
+Combines structural and view-dependent gradients for robust joint optimization of Gaussian positions and appearance, minimizing $\mathcal{L} = \lambda (\lambda_V \mathcal{L}_V + \lambda_S \mathcal{L}_S)$ [S9, unofficial].
 
 ### Single-view Mini-batch Strategy
-A training data sampling strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting where data is organized per viewpoint, and a mini-batch consists of data from only a single viewpoint [S10, unofficial]. For each viewpoint $i$ from 1 to $N$, the dataset $D_i$ containing pixel-color pairs $\{p_{ij}, C_{ij}\}$ is collected, organized into a collection $D = \{D_i\}$, and a mini-batch $B_{3DGS}$ is sampled from $D$ containing data from count=1 viewpoint [S10, unofficial].
+Organizes data per viewpoint and samples mini-batches from only a single viewpoint to improve training efficiency [S10, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training typically takes 15-30 minutes for standard datasets (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360) on modern GPUs, significantly faster than NeRF which can take hours [S5, unofficial]. The training loop involves iterative rasterization and backpropagation, with complexity scaling with the number of Gaussians and pixels [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians. The default maximum number of Gaussians is 5,000,000, but this can be unlimited [S3, unofficial]. Memory bandwidth is a bottleneck due to the large number of parameters [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the Replica dataset, SGS-SLAM achieves PSNR 34.15, SSIM 0.97, and LPIPS 0.096 [S1]. Compared to NeRF, Gaussian Splatting offers comparable image quality but significantly faster training and inference speeds [S2].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with GPU hardware; CPU execution is ~100x slower than GPU [S3, unofficial]. Resolution scaling is handled via downscale factors, increasing resolution every 3,000 steps [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, fast training, high fidelity [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Can produce "floaters" in empty space, sensitive to initialization, requires careful densification tuning [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training typically takes 15–60 minutes for standard datasets (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360, Tanks & Temples) on modern GPUs, significantly faster than NeRF which can take hours [S2][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians. Default limit is 5,000,000 Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. High-resolution scenes can exceed VRAM limits without optimization strategies [9264-optimization-and-training-efficiency.md].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves real-time rendering speeds (30–100+ FPS) at 1080p or higher, comparable to or better than NeRF in visual quality (PSNR, SSIM) [S2][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast training, real-time rendering, high visual fidelity, explicit representation allows for easy editing and compression [S2][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can produce "floaters" or artifacts in unstructured scenes; requires careful tuning of densification parameters; memory intensive for very large scenes [S2, 9264-optimization-and-training-efficiency.md].
+*   **Hardware:** Primarily GPU-accelerated (CUDA, Metal). CPU execution is ~100x slower [S3, S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [9]. The original method initializes positions using sparse point clouds from SfM tools like COLMAP [2, S2]. It is implemented in various frameworks, including an IPOL demo application for real-time single-image reconstruction [S2], and C++ libraries like OpenSplat [S3, unofficial] and Msplat [S4, unofficial]. It is used in SLAM, dynamic scene reconstruction, and human avatars [S1].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [9]. It builds on the concept of volumetric rendering but uses explicit 3D Gaussians as primitives instead of implicit neural fields. The method was developed to address the slow training and rendering speeds of NeRFs while maintaining high visual quality. It has since become a standard baseline for 3D reconstruction and novel view synthesis [S2][S5, unofficial].
 
